onebrain vendor patch 0003: RPC client-side async pipelining
(docs/perf.md §3 — overlapped chunked prefill)

Applies on top of 0001 + 0002. Client paths of ggml-rpc.cpp (plus one
additive declaration in ggml-rpc.h) only; the server side and the wire
format are untouched.

Correctness argument (also at the ob_rpc_pending block in the file): the
RPC server executes commands serially, in order, per connection, and
several commands carry no response (GRAPH_COMPUTE, GRAPH_RECOMPUTE,
SET_TENSOR, MEMSET_TENSOR). Deferring "acks" is therefore free: the
response of ANY later response-bearing command on the same socket proves
every command before it fully executed. The patch adds a per-socket
pending-command ledger (submitted/completed counters — the pending-ack
FIFO with implicit, batched acks), implements synchronize as a drain
(fence = one GET_DEVICE_MEMORY round trip when the ledger cannot prove
completion), events as markers into the ledger (record = snapshot
submitted; wait/synchronize = drain to the marker), async set/get/cpy
tensor entry points, and flips device caps to async=true, events=true —
which is exactly what llama.cpp's pipeline_parallel gate requires.
ggml_backend_rpc_pipeline_enable(false) reverts the caps advertisement
process-wide (contexts created afterwards run without parallel copies) —
the hook behind OneBrain's `[perf] prefill_overlap = false` constructed
M3 baseline.
Failures compose with 0002: a failed fence marks the socket dead and the
error surfaces on whichever status-returning call drains or runs next.

